/**
 * Tell the person who asked for this run that CI is actually running, and where to
 * watch it.
 *
 * A person's `/agent` on a pull request is handed to validation, and the wait is
 * minutes long; a person who typed a command and saw nothing happen has no way to
 * tell a queued run from a broken workflow. So validation itself says the one fact
 * that is true while the run goes -- and nothing more. It does NOT name the agent
 * that will work next, because the result decides that: checks that fail dispatch
 * the engineer who opened the pull request, not the agent the person named. The
 * old workflow-side comment did promise the agent, and said it even when CI was
 * never going to run -- validation reuses a run that already finished, so the most
 * common case was a promise about a wait that did not exist (#271).
 *
 * `askedByPerson` only. An agent's push already knows its validation is running --
 * the tool that started it says so -- and a comment on every push would be noise on
 * the thread the agent is working in. A reused completed run says nothing either:
 * the person's own command is the record that it was asked for, and the verdict
 * that reuse returns is seconds old, so there is no wait left to describe.
 */
function notifyCiWaiting(repo: string, number: string, askedByPerson: boolean, runId: number): void {
  if (!askedByPerson) return;
  const runUrl = `https://github.com/${repo}/actions/runs/${runId}`;
